# Global Best Practices

This project follows strict guidelines for performance, accessibility, and code maintainability.

## Image Optimization

- **Use `next/image` Component**: Always use the `next/image` component instead of standard `<img>` tags.
- **Size Appropriately**: Use the `sizes` prop to help the browser select the correct image size.
    - **Responsive Grid Example**:
      ```typescript
      <div className="grid-element relative h-64"> {/* Parent must be relative for fill */}
        <Image
          fill
          src="/example.png"
          alt="Descriptive alt text"
          sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, (max-width: 1200px) 50vw, 33vw"
          className="object-cover"
        />
      </div>
      ```
- **Avoid Layout Shift**: Always specify `width` and `height` or use `fill` with a parent container that has `position: relative` (or fixed/absolute).
- **Lazy Loading**: Images are lazy-loaded by default. Use `priority` for LCP (Largest Contentful Paint) images (e.g., hero section).
- **Accessibility**: Always provide a descriptive `alt` attribute. Use `alt=""` for decorative images.

## DOM Size Optimization

- **Avoid Excessive Wrappers**: Don't create extra `div` elements solely for styling (e.g., backgrounds, borders) if pseudo-elements (`::before`, `::after`) can achieve the same result.
- **Use CSS Utility Classes**:
    - Use `.dotted-bg` for radial gradient backgrounds instead of separate wrapper divs.
    - Use `.grid-item-visual` for visual containers.
    - Use `.grid-item-content` for content wrappers.
    - Use `.max-w-container` for section max-width constraints.

## Accessibility (A11y)

- **Semantic HTML**: Use semantic tags (`<section>`, `<article>`, `<nav>`, `<button>`) appropriately.
- **Accessible Names**: Ensure all interactive elements (buttons, links, inputs) have an accessible name via text content, `aria-label`, or `aria-labelledby`.
- **Decorative Elements**: Hide decorative icons using `aria-hidden="true"`.
- **Contrast**: Ensure text meets WCAG AA contrast ratios (4.5:1 for normal text). Avoid light grays like `text-zinc-400` on white backgrounds; prefer `text-zinc-500` or darker.
- **Heading Hierarchy**: Maintain sequential heading levels (`h1` -> `h2` -> `h3`).

## SEO & Structured Data

- **Metadata**: Use the Next.js `Metadata` API for all pages.
    - Define `title`, `description`, `openGraph`, `twitter`, and `alternates` (canonical).
    - Example:
      ```typescript
      export const metadata: Metadata = {
        title: "My Page Title",
        description: "Concise summary...",
        openGraph: { ... },
      };
      ```
- **Rich Snippets (JSON-LD)**: Use `application/ld+json` script tags to provide structured data.
    - Implement for: `Organization`, `Person`, `WebSite`, `BreadcrumbList`, `Article`, or `Product` where applicable.
    - Use valid schema.org types.
    - Validate using Google's Rich Results Test.
- **Semantic Structure**: Ensure standard HTML5 landmarks (`<header>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`, `<aside>`) are used to help search engines understand page structure.
- **Canonical URLs**: Always specify a canonical URL to prevent duplicate content issues.

## CSS & Tailwind

- **Extract Common Patterns**: Identify repeated complex class strings and extract them to `globals.css` using `@layer components` or standard CSS classes if they reduce DOM nodes.
- **Motion Preferences**: Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` media query for animations.

## Next.js App Router

- **Manifest**: Link web manifests correctly via the `metadata` object, not manual `<link>` tags in `layout.tsx`.
- **File Structure**: Keep routes in `app/` and reusable components in `components/`.
